pope francis has refused to accept the  resignation of one of germany's top  catholic bishops  of the handling of a sex abuse scandal  cardinal reinhardt marx  offered his resignation in a letter to  the pope last week  marx who is the bishop of munich said he  had a shared responsibility for what he  called  the catastrophe of sexual abuse by  clerics  marx is not accused of committing abuse  himself a study found that nearly 4 000  children  mostly boys were sexually assaulted by  german clergymen  between 1946 and 2014.  uw's religious affairs correspondent  martin guck joins us now for more on  this story martin put this into  perspective for us why has the pope  refused cardinal  marx's resignation  well i mean it's very hard to say why i  mean what are the reasons behind what is  quite clear  is that in terms of  you know what was a very very very uh  big public relations stunt we might call  it i mean it was really a  very big deal a week ago this can only  be read as a rebuke  in the sense that uh you know now we're  talking about offering a resignation  whereas of last week  it really looked like you know he was  really just presenting a resignation  closing  the door in a position of power now what  we have is the pope essentially saying  no i mean you're going to stay where  where you are  um of course hard to say why but  clearly it has a lot to do with  institutional integrity  so the game is clearly not at an end and  now it is for  cardinal marx essentially to say  something because this  obviously cannot be the last word so  what could be the next step then for the  archbishop of munich he's one of the  catholic church's most senior figures  and a known liberal cleric  it's it's really very very difficult to  say and we should underline that  everything here is  speculation i mean mainly because the  story i mean  is a pretty dramatic story and this is a  dramatic twist in an already dramatic  story  but i think that we can expect one of a  few things we can expect  simply marx to go back uh from where we  came from from where he came from  which is a prominent position and  continue his job without basically  addressing  uh the response of the pope that would  be very very strange particularly  because he's a very important public  figure  and because a lot of what he brought to  the table i mean what was including his  letter of resignation had a lot to do  with the sexual abuse scandal and  essentially a  the accusation of a broad cover-up the  next question would be whether he would  take a more  senior be offer a more senior position  to actually deal with the things  that he has expressed his satisfaction  with that is not something  that you know it's clear but that could  be one way in which the vatican tries to  essentially uh go over uh  the qualms and the reservations  expressed by marx in his  uh in his letter the other thing that  obviously is on the table as well  is a clear break within or a clear clear  tension within the catholic church of  germany in which a very very  conservative figure like  uh wilkie in in colin who has been under  great pressure has refused to resign and  marx who has not been accused of any  kind of uh  uh you know missteps or any kind of  abuse  has actually taken the resignation of  saying the state of the church  is such that the only way to actually  move forward is  for actually for me to resign so this  tension i think will be  brought back to to to be visible will be  brought back to  to the top of the agenda as far as does  this situation is that  is this a german problem or does it  point to a wider crisis of the catholic  church  well i mean last week the american the  american specialized media  uh was abbas with the question as to  what was next for colonel marx and what  was  he going to be offered obviously he's  not a household name  in the us or in the rest of the catholic  world in the way that it is in germany  because he's here also a fairly  important  public figure nonetheless he is a  powerhouse within the progressive and  reformist  wing of the church and he has a very  close relation with the pope and he has  a massive footprint in vatican politics  over the last  five or six years our religious affairs  correspondent martin guck there in  berlin  thank you martin you're welcome  you
 

